EXT. CANAL LOCK - LOWER BASIN - DUSK

1824. A narrow barge squeezed in a stone lock. Water churns. Lanterns swing on posts. Ropes creak.

LOCKKEEPER HOSEA DRUMMOND, 50s, broad, in a tarred coat, cranks the sluice. His daughter CLEMENTINE DRUMMOND, 12, perches on the gate beam with a coil of rope. At the stern of the barge, CAPTAIN EZEKIEL ARDEN, 40s, tall hat, cane, looks displeased. His wife, MADAME PERPETUA ARDEN, 40s, silk veil, grips the rail, glaring at the churning water and murmuring to the horses on the towpath.

HOSEA
Steady now. She'll rise.

EZEKIEL
You said twenty minutes.

HOSEA
I said it'd be about twenty.

CLEMENTINE
It's been twenty-two, sir.

EZEKIEL
Impertinent child.

CLEMENTINE
Accurate child.

Perpetua lifts her veil. Stares at Clementine. A flicker of amusement she smothers at once.

PERPETUA
Ezekiel. Let the man work.

EZEKIEL
I have a train to meet.

HOSEA
No train this side of Wickham.

EZEKIEL
There is a train.

HOSEA
There's a promise of one.

The water surges. The barge shudders and lifts. A rope snaps. The barge yaws toward the wall. Clementine scrambles along the beam, grabs the loose end, hauls with both hands. Hosea spins the crank backward. Ezekiel stumbles, cane clattering. Perpetua clutches the rail and shouts at the horses. They back and the barge straightens.

OFFICER (V.O.)
(from the towpath, distant)
You there! In the lock! Halt in the Crown's name!

Everyone freezes. Water keeps rising. Hosea looks at the bank. Four men in coats at the far lamp. A constable's whistle.

EZEKIEL
(quietly, not at all arrogant now)
Hosea. Open the upper gate.

HOSEA
Sir?

EZEKIEL
Open it. Now.

CLEMENTINE
Papa?

Hosea looks at Ezekiel. At Perpetua, pale. At Clementine. He sets his jaw. Cranks the upper sluice. The gate groans open.

HOSEA
Hold on to something.

The water roars. The barge shoots forward into the dark channel, taking the lantern light with it, and the officers' shouts vanish behind.
